  <h2 id="money-check">Money check before you pay</h2>

  <p>Our strongest suggestion: keep what you pay for any one course or degree near a tenth of everything your family can spend on your education, and treat anything above about a fifth as a warning that needs proof from that exact course. This line does not cover free or very cheap online learning, such as YouTube lessons or a low-priced Udemy or Coursera course. It matters most for bootcamps and paid certificates, which can cost 30 to 100 times more than similar lessons online.</p>
  <p>An illustration, not a real fee: take a family education budget of Rs 4 lakh. A tenth is Rs 40,000 and a fifth is Rs 80,000, so a Rs 1.5 lakh data science bootcamp sits far above the warning line. Keep the rest for a laptop, practice tools, books and a reserve while you look for work.</p>
  <p>Before paying, ask for the syllabus, the last update date, what past learners built and the refund terms. This is general information, not financial advice, so avoid an education loan for a short course and get any loan terms in writing.</p>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Start with the daily work you can stand. Then choose the employer type you will aim for and the one skill that moves you into a higher band. <strong>Proof of work in that skill is what makes the higher band reachable, though no path guarantees it.</strong>
  </p>

